es, whatever the agent's reason (a question, a status report, a live `AskUserQuestion` call). An autonomous run notifies its parent once per held turn end as creel mail to the orch, and once per permission or input dialog its pane shows past 60 s, and an interactive run is answered in its pane; a line typed into a run's pane goes through `lym creel post`, `lym creel inject` or `lym creel interrupt`, never a shuttle verb. Background work is read off the Stop payload's `background_tasks` first, with the transcript as the fallback when the payload carries no list; a turn end with outstanding work is waiting, not held, unless its only outstanding work is non-awaited shells, which a gated run holds at once with an output file missing, a gated run with every output file present counts done once they pass the shell wait bound after telling the agent to read them, and an ungated run holds once they pass the shell wait bound. `Attach` waits on a held run, and on a legacy `asking` record whose strand is still live, instead of respawning beside it. `died` is narrow: it means a strand reed STILL TRACKS has a pane that is not alive, the provider never came up inside the startup window, or the provider's process is proven gone behind a live pane shell (by the process registry, or a process-ending `SessionEnd` line past the wait's first liveness tick), with `done` instead when every output file exists. Reed's strand table losing the strand entirely — a `reed down`/`remove`, a deleted or rebuilt `reed.json`, a worktree renamed under an in-flight run — is reported as a mechanism failure carrying the run's identity, never as `died`, because reed's bookkeeping going away says nothing about the agent, whose process is often still working in its pane; the exception is a handle whose own `Stop` removed the strand, which ends `died` and is recorded as stopped unless the files show the run done. No guardrail denies the in-process `Agent` tool: a subagent or fork shows in the session's own 